[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" "question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" "question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/id #uuid "4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-156682" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x+y=1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" "answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" "answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" "answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" "answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/id #uuid "7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/id #uuid "611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/id #uuid "27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/id #uuid "c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/id #uuid "3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/id #uuid "b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-157293" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" "answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" "answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" "answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" "answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/id #uuid "aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/id #uuid "e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/id #uuid "c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/id #uuid "9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/id #uuid "c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/id #uuid "659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156650" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" "answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" "answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" "answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" "answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/id #uuid "3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I  and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/id #uuid "66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/id #uuid "7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/id #uuid "f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/id #uuid "daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/id #uuid "4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36"] ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-156682"] ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x+y=1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$"] ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796"] ["answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/id #uuid "7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9"] ["answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/id #uuid "611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963"] ["answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/id #uuid "27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190"] ["answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/id #uuid "c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99"] ["answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/id #uuid "3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0"] ["answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0" :answer/value "IV only"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/correct "answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/choices "answer-7c410928-3ce9-5370-862c-096ef43c12b9"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/choices "answer-611c61c9-2060-5668-8f8b-b62583834963"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/choices "answer-27b679e1-d8ce-51e5-9729-049a3614b190"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/choices "answer-c3520d96-4f07-5cf1-8505-57df5ffddb99"] ["answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" :answer-field/choices "answer-3aaac4dc-8cc1-5e8d-a4d0-c9f1414c29d0"] ["question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796"] [17592186119926 :knowledge-point/questions "question-4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36"] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/id #uuid "b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6"] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-157293"] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$"] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493"] ["answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/id #uuid "aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a"] ["answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/id #uuid "e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c"] ["answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c" :answer/value "I and IV only"] ["answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/id #uuid "c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c"] ["answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c" :answer/value "I, II, and III only"] ["answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/id #uuid "9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74"] ["answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/id #uuid "c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997"] ["answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997" :answer/value "None of the statements are true"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/correct "answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/choices "answer-aaf8be50-2dcb-53c5-b934-278e3226720a"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/choices "answer-e9d96ee1-5b25-51c2-816d-45db597b2b2c"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/choices "answer-c017120c-6cc9-5db6-9d68-80c622f3d73c"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/choices "answer-9461a998-4cc2-5192-a97d-04cf50c8bd74"] ["answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" :answer-field/choices "answer-c7873f66-26a0-59de-9a80-d3b3617f5997"] ["question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493"] [17592186119926 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6"] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/id #uuid "659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8"] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156650"] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$"] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62"] ["answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/id #uuid "3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329"] ["answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329" :answer/value "I  and IV only"] ["answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/id #uuid "66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea"] ["answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/id #uuid "7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3"] ["answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3" :answer/value "II and IV only"] ["answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/id #uuid "f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100"] ["answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/id #uuid "daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326"] ["answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326" :answer/value "IV only"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/correct "answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/choices "answer-3ebd4668-8267-5d3a-b4f4-d5cbfd1c4329"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/choices "answer-66e85388-f612-522c-bc5e-3676641770ea"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/choices "answer-7a79477f-76f5-54d3-8cfb-e2dac391c8d3"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/choices "answer-f01111f4-c13f-5cd1-8954-a88c666d6100"] ["answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" :answer-field/choices "answer-daa59206-3a60-5124-961d-56dd6e50b326"] ["question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62"] [17592186119926 :knowledge-point/questions "question-659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8"] [17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$"] [17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"] [17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"]] "retractions" [[17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$"] [17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"] [17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"]]}]